  207|   119M|    // All of these biases were derived by measuring the cost of coding
  208|   119M|    // a zero vs coding a one on any given coefficient position, or, in
  209|   119M|    // the case of the EOB bias, the cost of coding the block with
  210|   119M|    // the chosen EOB (rounding to one) vs rounding to zero and continuing
  211|   119M|    // to choose a new EOB. This was done over several clips, with the
  212|   119M|    // average of the bit costs taken over all blocks in the set, and a new
  213|   119M|    // bias derived via the method outlined in Jean-Marc Valin's
  214|   119M|    // Journal of Dubious Theoretical Results[1], aka:
  215|   119M|    //
  216|   119M|    // lambda = ln(2) / 6.0
  217|   119M|    // threshold = 0.5 + (lambda * avg_rate_diff) / 2.0
